WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.860
Medication management is key for all individuals living with diabetes

2
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.880
when they become sick, or on the off chance that they do become sick.

3
00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:22.860
And helping our clients understand that, yes, it may be the safer

4
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.900
option to stop a medicine, and making sure that they understand

5
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.840
which medications to pause, which medications to continue,

6
00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:32.880
and which medications to adjust.

7
00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:43.830
So let's talk a little bit about SADMANs and GLP-1 receptor

8
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.620
agonists and why they're really important to

9
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.300
help our clients identify,

10
00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.760
because I think that's where health professionals like us can make a really huge

11
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.580
impact in sick day management with people living with diabetes.

12
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.360
So SADMANs stands for sulfonylureas, ACE

13
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.300
inhibitors, diuretics, metformin,

14
00:01:05.041 --> 00:01:08.320
A2Rbs, non-steroidal anti-inflammatories,

15
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.940
the ever-increasing SGLT2 inhibitors, and then we add on the

16
00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:14.980
GLP-1s as well at the end.

17
00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.180
So we all spend, regardless of our setting, we all spend so much of our

18
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.560
professional lives talking to our clients about

19
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.500
adherence and making sure that they take their medicines regularly, don't miss any

20
00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:29.150
doses, stay consistent, it's the best thing for your health.

21
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.370
But when someone's sick, we actually need to flip that around, and that

22
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.340
can be a little bit confronting for both health professionals and the

23
00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:38.640
clients that we're speaking to.

24
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.420
But sometimes the safest, most evidence-based thing that a person

25
00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.930
can do is to pause a medicine temporarily

26
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.820
while they're sick, particularly when you've got a situation where there's

27
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.300
vomiting, diarrhea, and we're struggling to keep things down and

28
00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.920
dehydration is a real risk.

29
00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.240
Now, this is not poor adherence. This is smart

30
00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:04.200
adherence. It's about using our medicines well and about also

31
00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.300
knowing when to take them and to avoid potential

32
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:08.440
hospitalizations.

33
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.920
So when someone is sick, the dehydration combined

34
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.930
with reduced oral intake can change how medicines work in

35
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:23.470
someone's body.

36
00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.060
Continuing certain drugs, and those ones that we've definitely just spoken about,

37
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:31.540
can actually increase the incidence of acute kidney injury,

38
00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:35.340
hypoglycemia, diabetic ketoacidosis, as

39
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.220
well as the euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis, which we're seeing a

40
00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.940
big increase with the links to SGLT2 inhibitors.

41
00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:47.510
And continuing drugs can actually worsen dehydration

42
00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.530
as well, which is something that we're really essentially trying to avoid.

43
00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:53.590
Now, pausing is really important.

44
00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:58.049
And I use the word "pause" because we don't want people to stop

45
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.880
and then not restart. So when you talk about pausing a medicine,

46
00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.000
also letting the person know they should restart that medicine

47
00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.660
24 to 48 hours after they're feeling better.

48
00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.560
And so that's why it's pausing and not stopping.
